<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Limes Germanicus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhine-Danube frontier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Limes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> was the fortified frontier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the land between them. It consisted of forts, watchtowers, walls and palisades guarding Roman provinces against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>. It is a prominent example of the defensive frontier systems that shaped Roman military strategy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus.jpg" alt="Limes Germanicus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Weinandt · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saalburg_-_Haupteingang_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Limes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> is the modern name for the frontier fortifications that bounded the Roman provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a>, Germania Superior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>. Where no river served, the line was typically an earth bank and ditch with a wooden palisade and watchtowers at intervals, with linked forts behind. At its height it ran from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> outlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to near Regensburg on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, 568 kilometres in all, with at least 60 forts and 900 watchtowers.</p><p>It had three parts: the Lower Germanic Limes along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a> from Katwijk, the Upper Germanic Limes from Rheinbrohl across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> to the Main and south to Lorch, and the Rhaetian Limes from Lorch to Eining on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The most heavily guarded stretch was the land corridor between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and the Danube at Regensburg, where large groups could move without water transport.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Augustus began to fortify the border after the defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a> in 9 CE. Under Vespasian a series of advances began: about 74 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> was partly annexed, and about 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> pushed out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and enclosed the new land within a frontier of blockhouses with larger forts behind. One blockhouse grew into the Saalburg fort on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a>. The frontier was later given a continuous wooden palisade from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, probably by Hadrian.</p><p>Either Hadrian or, more probably, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> then marked out a new line further forward, with a palisade, ditch and mound known as the Pfahlgraben and a stone wall, the Teufelsmauer, running to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Pressure from beyond the frontier grew in the later second century, and almost the whole district east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and north of the Danube was lost around 250. After invasions in the late third century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> fell back on a line along the Rhine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iller">Iller</a> and Danube, supported by fast river boats.</p><p>The line was never meant to stop whole peoples. Near the towers it stayed open to traders and to people coming to live or work in the empire, and its purpose was to control that traffic. It gave early warning, deterred small raids and let the garrisons meet an attack while the enemy was still near the border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The remains of the Upper Germanic and Rhaetian Limes were inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2005 as part of the Frontiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and the Lower Germanic Limes followed in 2021. The Saalburg, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, is a reconstructed fort and museum of the frontier.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Limes Germanicus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Rhine-Danube frontier</b>, fixed after Arminius ambushed three legions under Publius Quinctilius Varus in the Teutoburg Forest, marked the northern limit of Roman expansion, with lasting effects on European history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>These troubles may have influenced his plan for the wall, as well as his construction of frontier boundaries known as limes in other areas of the empire, such as the <b>Limes Germanicus</b> in Germany.</blockquote><a href="/work/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Domitian fought the Chatti in 83 to 85 and pushed the frontier forward along the Taunus and Wetterau, the line later fortified as the <b>Limes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/chatti">Chatti</a></li></ul></section>
